  <h2>Kreung in one sentence</h2>
  <p>Kreung (<span lang="km">{khmer.kreung}</span>, sometimes spelled <em>kroeung</em>) is a paste of fresh herbs and aromatics, pounded together by hand, that gives many Cambodian dishes their flavor.</p>
  <p>If you've eaten Cambodian food, you've probably tasted it. It's in soups, stir-fries, curries and marinades. Think of it as the flavor base a Cambodian cook reaches for first.</p>

  <h2>The six ingredients in our kreung</h2>
  <p>Families make kreung a little differently, and there are several kinds. Ours uses six ingredients. Here they are, with their Khmer names.</p>
  <ul>
    {kreungIngredients.map((i) => (
      <li><strong>{i.en}</strong> (<span lang="km">{i.km}</span>). {notes[i.en]}</li>
    ))}
  </ul>
  <p>Put together, they taste bright, citrusy, earthy and savory all at once. There's no single loud flavor. That balance is the point.</p>

  <h2>How kreung is made</h2>
  <p>The traditional way is with a mortar and pestle. You slice the tough herbs thin, then pound them a handful at a time until they break down into a thick, rough paste. Pounding crushes the fibers and releases oils in a way a blender can't quite match.</p>
  <p>It takes time and muscle. That's part of why kreung feels like home cooking. Many Cambodian Americans remember a parent or grandparent pounding it in the kitchen. On our homepage you can try a little "pound the kreung" game to see how the six ingredients come together.</p>

  <h2>Different kinds of kreung</h2>
  <p>Cambodian cooks make more than one kind of kreung, and they're often named by color. The color depends on which ingredients lead. Turmeric gives a golden paste. Using more of the green parts of lemongrass gives a greener one. Some versions add dried chilies for a red paste.</p>
  <p>The kind we use for our BBQ is golden from turmeric and packed with lemongrass.</p>

  <h2>How kreung is used in Cambodian cooking</h2>
  <p>Kreung shows up all over the Cambodian table:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Soups and stews.</strong> Stirred into simmering broths to build flavor fast.</li>
    <li><strong>Curries.</strong> Fried in oil, then cooked with coconut milk.</li>
    <li><strong>Steamed dishes.</strong> Amok, a well-known Cambodian dish, uses kreung for its fragrant flavor.</li>
    <li><strong>Marinades.</strong> Rubbed onto meat before grilling. This is how we use it.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How we use kreung at Kreung LA</h2>
  <p>Every meat we serve soaks in kreung before it touches the grill. That includes our lemongrass chicken, beef skewers, pork belly and short rib. Then we cook over fire, so the herbs char a little at the edges. You get smoke, citrus and spice in the same bite.</p>
  <p>We serve it with rice, coconut corn, pickled vegetables and our house sauces. The sides keep things fresh and crunchy next to the rich, grilled meat.</p>
